----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, Q: d8-d5, Q: d8-e7, Q: d8-f6, Q: d8-g5, Q: d8-h4, P: e4-e3, K: e8-d7, K: e8-e7, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: f8-b4, B: f8-a3, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, N: g8-e7, N: g8-f6, N: g8-h6, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nSeveral moves are worth considering. The pawn push P: g7-g5 stands out as it opens lines for the bishop on f8 or potentially prepares kingside expansion, but it also weakens the dark squares around the black king. Alternatively, developing the knight with N: g8-f6 develops a piece, controls central squares (e4, d5), and supports future central play — a very natural and strong developing move. Another option is Q: d8-d6, eyeing the weakened e4-pawn and possibly preparing pressure along the 6th rank, but it may be premature without full development.\n\nThe knight move N: g8-f6 is particularly appealing because:\n- It develops a minor piece to an active square.\n- It exerts pressure on the center (e4 and d5).\n- It doesn't weaken the king's position.\n- It prepares potential follow-ups like ...Bf8-e7 or ...0-0 if safe.\n\nOther knight options like N: b8-c6 also develop, but c6 is slightly less optimal due to potential blocking of the c7-pawn and less influence over e4 compared to f6.\n\nReason:  \nN: g8-f6 is a principled developing move that increases activity, contests the center, and avoids unnecessary pawn weaknesses. It fits well with classical opening principles and improves Black’s coordination in a position where White has some central presence (e.g., queen on h4 indirectly, knight on c3, pawn on e4).\n\nAnswer: N: g8-f6"}
