4, g4, etc.), and retreating it to d7 loses tempo and influence.\n\nQ: d8-d7 is passive; it blocks the open file for the rook and doesn’t contribute to immediate threats or development.\n\nN: b8-d7 is the only move that actively develops a minor piece toward the center, improves connectivity, and follows opening principles. It brings the undeveloped queenside knight into play, eyes the central squares like c5 and e6, and prepares potential ...e5 or ...c5 breaks later.\n\nReason:  \nN: b8-d7 is the best developing move, consistent with opening strategy, while the other options either waste time, block pieces, or inappropriately move the king.\n\nAnswer: N: b8-d7'}
